            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a gap, then the Latin for a moment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: incipiō, incipere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "incipiō, incipere — \"to begin\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mīlitēs mūrum aedificāre incēpērunt. The soldiers began to build a wall."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Perfect incēpī. With an infinitive: incipit loquī, he begins to speak. English incipient."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "incipiō is built on capiō, \"to take\", with in- (Lewis & Short): to lay hold of a task, to take it in hand. English incipient comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
