          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "intermittō, intermittere — \"to break off, to interrupt\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Propter pluviam labōrem intermīsimus. Because of the rain we broke off the work."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "intermittō, intermittere, intermīsī, intermissum: to put a gap into something. Caesar uses it of a battle or a march that is broken off."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "intermittō is built on mittō, \"to send, to let go\", with inter, \"between\" (Lewis & Short): to leave a gap. English intermission and intermittent come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
